In international flower trade, time and temperature determine value. A strong cold chain keeps roses in an almost dormant state until they reach the buyer, preserving freshness, firmness, and presentation.

## The risk of poor thermal control

Without stable temperature management, roses can suffer from:

- Temperature spikes
- Moisture accumulation
- Mechanical compression
- Dehydration
- Premature opening
- Thermal shock

## Lladeal's role in logistics efficiency

Lladeal combines technical packaging, disciplined timing, and post-harvest temperature control to reduce the weak points of traditional flower logistics.

## Advanced conservation and transport techniques

### Pre-cooling before shipment

Forced-air cooling removes field heat quickly and helps the core of the box reach a stable shipping temperature before dispatch.

### Structural packaging design

Moisture-resistant boxes and internal support systems improve load resistance during stacking and air transport.

### Internal microclimate control

Micro-perforated materials and protective sheets regulate humidity and reduce the risk of petal burn, condensation, or suffocation.

### Real-time transit monitoring

Temperature and humidity loggers provide traceability during critical routes and help identify hidden breaks in the cold chain.

### Dispatch coordination

Harvesting, packing, and loading are synchronized with flight schedules to reduce idle time in hot loading zones.

## Benefits for importers

With better cold chain discipline, importers gain lower waste, higher consistency, and more operational predictability during peak sales periods.

## Conclusion

Cold chain management is one of the main pillars of premium flower export. When logistics are treated as a quality process instead of just transport, roses arrive fresher and perform better in every market.
